## Metadata

Read `width`, `height`, and `format` without decoding pixel data:

// Create an error
const err = new Error("Something went wrong");
console.log(Bun.inspect(err, { colors: true }));
```

The output is a syntax-highlighted preview of the source code where the error occurred, along with the error message and stack trace.

const obj = { foo: "bar" };
const str = Bun.inspect(obj);
// => '{\nfoo: "bar" \n}'

const arr = new Uint8Array([1, 2, 3]);
const str = Bun.inspect(arr);
// => "Uint8Array(3) [ 1, 2, 3 ]"
```

### `Bun.inspect.custom`

The symbol Bun uses to implement `Bun.inspect`. Override it to customize how your objects are printed. It is identical to `util.inspect.custom` in Node.js.

console.log(
  Bun.inspect.table(
    [
      { a: 1, b: 2, c: 3 },
      { a: 4, b: 5, c: 6 },
    ],
    {
      colors: true,
    },
  ),
);
```

